## Why This Is Asked
This tests fixed-length path DP and path reconstruction in graphs, a core DP pattern that shows depth beyond standard knapsack.

## Key Concepts
- DP on path length t
- State transition using edges
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time and space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEdgePath(n, edges, k) {
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) dp[v][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const e of edges) {
      const {u,v,w} = e;
      const prev = dp[u][t-1];
      if (prev !== NEG) {
        const cand = prev + w;
        if (cand > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = cand;
          par[v][t] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let bestV = 0, best = NEG;
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) if (dp[v][k] > best) { best = dp[v][k]; bestV = v; }
  const path = [];
  let cur = bestV;
  for (let t=k; t>=1; t--) { path.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; }
  path.push(cur);
  path.reverse();
  return {path, weight: best};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this change if edges could have negative weights? How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests classic DP with an additional dimension (at most k intervals) and reconstruction—a common pattern in scheduling optimization problems.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint (at most k intervals)
- Predecessor p(i) computed via binary search on sorted end times
- DP table with an extra dimension tracking the number of selected intervals
- Backtracking reconstruction to output the chosen jobs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueKJobs(jobs, k) {
  const sorted = jobs.map((j, idx) => ({ ...j, idx })).sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = sorted.length;
  const ends = sorted.map(j => j.end);

  const p = (i) => {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (ends[mid] <= sorted[i].start) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return hi;
  };

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const prev = p(i - 1) + 1;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], dp[prev][t - 1] + sorted[i - 1].value);
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let t = k;
  for (let i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
    if (dp[i][t] !== dp[i - 1][t]) {
      selected.push(sorted[i - 1]);
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to model non-crossing, higher-arity structures with DP and reconstruct a solution.

## Key Concepts

- DP state dp[i][t] for i and t
- Non-crossing triple formation
- Reconstruction via backpointers
